---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import Card from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/CardGrid.astro";
import Code from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Code.astro";
import Note from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Note.astro";
import ThemedImage from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/ThemedImage.astro";
import { u } from "../../data/nav";
// Two pictures for the example: a light screenshot and its dark twin, drawn here as SVG.
const shot = (ground: string, ink: string, bar: string) => `data:image/svg+xml,${encodeURIComponent(`<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 100 56"><rect width="100" height="56" fill="${ground}"/><rect x="8" y="8" width="40" height="5" fill="${ink}"/><rect x="8" y="19" width="84" height="3" fill="${bar}"/><rect x="8" y="26" width="84" height="3" fill="${bar}"/><rect x="8" y="33" width="60" height="3" fill="${bar}"/><rect x="8" y="42" width="24" height="6" fill="#D9412B"/></svg>`)}`;
const light = shot("#FFFFFF", "#141414", "#D9DAD6");
const dark = shot("#161615", "#F2F2EE", "#3A3A37");
---
<Docs title="Cards" status="experimental" lede="Entries on a list page, hung as exhibits: a black panel with the entry's figure or its own complex, then the title, a meta line, and a summary.">
  <Note kind="info">Every title and venue on this page is example content.</Note>

  <h2>A list page</h2>
  <p>The first card leads: it spans the row and, with room, sets its panel beside the text and its title in wood type, with the subtitle under the name. The others share the rows, up to three to a row.</p>
  <CardGrid class="ply-wide">
    <Card lead href="#" title="Example title: Higher-order message passing on cell complexes" meta={["Example Venue 2026", "Oral"]} badge="Top 3 paper" summary="A one-sentence summary of the paper, set in the text face under the meta line." />
    <Card href="#" title="Example title: a second paper in the same year" meta={["Example Workshop 2026"]} badge="Preprint" summary="Each card without a figure draws a complex seeded from its own title." />
    <Card href="#" title="Example title: an earlier preprint" meta={["arXiv preprint"]} summary="The panel repeats the title's link for a pointer; a keyboard reaches the title only." tags={["Topology", "Graphs"]} />
  </CardGrid>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<CardGrid>
  <Card lead href="/papers/x/" title="Name: what it does" meta={["ICLR 2026", "Oral"]} badge="Top 3 paper" summary="...">
    <Picture slot="figure" src={figure} alt="" />
  </Card>
  <Card href="/papers/y/" title="..." meta={["Preprint"]} />
</CardGrid>`} />
  <ul>
    <li>Put the figure in the <code>figure</code> slot with empty alt text: the title names the entry, and the panel is a second way to the same page. Without a figure, the card draws a Vietoris-Rips complex seeded from its title, so each entry has its own; <code>seed</code> picks another.</li>
    <li><code>badge</code> hangs wall labels on the panel. They carry their own pale ground and edge (<code>.ply-label--flag</code>, the <code>flag</code> tokens), so they read on a white screenshot and a black panel, in both themes.</li>
    <li>The grid shares a row between fewer cards than it has columns, so a year with two papers sets two wide cards, and a card alone sets like a lead.</li>
    <li><code>level</code> sets the title's heading level (3 by default), and <code>link</code> passes attributes to the title's link, such as data attributes for analytics.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="aligned">Aligned rows and three tracks</h2>
  <p>Cards with summaries of different lengths leave titles and link rows at different heights across a row. <code>.ply-cards--aligned</code> lines the parts up with subgrid, from 48rem: each card takes seven rows of the grid (panel, title, meta, summary, authors, tags, links), so the parts of one row of cards start on shared lines and the row is as tall as its tallest part. An unused row collapses. <code>.ply-cards--tracks</code> keeps the grid at exactly three tracks from 80rem, whatever a row holds, so a card is the same size on every row. The lead, or a card alone, then puts its panel across two tracks and its text in the third, with its title a step smaller.</p>
  <CardGrid class="ply-wide ply-cards--aligned ply-cards--tracks">
    <Card lead href="#" title="Example title: a lead card across two tracks" meta={["Example Venue 2026", "Oral"]} summary="With three tracks the lead's panel ends where the second track ends.">
      <p class="ply-card__authors">A. Author, <span class="ply-me">John Doe</span>, B. Author</p>
      <p class="ply-card__links"><a href="#">Paper</a><a href="#">Code</a><a href="#">BibTeX</a></p>
    </Card>
    <Card href="#" title="Example title: a short summary" meta={["Example Workshop 2026"]} summary="One line.">
      <p class="ply-card__authors"><span class="ply-me">John Doe</span>, C. Author</p>
      <p class="ply-card__links"><a href="#">Paper</a><a href="#">BibTeX</a></p>
    </Card>
    <Card href="#" title="Example title: a second card whose title takes two lines in a narrow track" meta={["Example Venue 2025"]} summary="A longer summary that takes three lines in its track, so the authors and links of the cards beside it start on the same lines.">
      <p class="ply-card__authors">D. Author, <span class="ply-me">John Doe</span>, E. Author</p>
      <p class="ply-card__links"><a href="#">Paper</a><a href="#">Data</a><a href="#">BibTeX</a></p>
    </Card>
    <Card href="#" title="Example title: an earlier preprint" meta={["arXiv preprint"]} summary="Two lines of summary, set between the others." tags={["Topology"]}>
      <p class="ply-card__authors"><span class="ply-me">John Doe</span>, F. Author</p>
      <p class="ply-card__links"><a href="#">arXiv</a><a href="#">BibTeX</a></p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<CardGrid class="ply-cards--aligned ply-cards--tracks">
  <Card href="/papers/x/" title="..." meta={["ICLR 2026"]} summary="...">
    <p class="ply-card__authors">A. Author, <span class="ply-me">John Doe</span></p>
    <p class="ply-card__links"><a href="/x.pdf">Paper</a><a href="/papers/x/#cite">BibTeX</a></p>
  </Card>
</CardGrid>`} />
  <ul>
    <li><code>.ply-card__authors</code> is the authors line in 14px caption gray, with the owner's name, in <code>.ply-me</code>, set darker. <code>.ply-card__links</code> is a row of small links, 24px tall, 44px under a finger. Both go in the card's default slot.</li>
    <li>The classes work together or apart. Without subgrid, the last block of each card sits at the card's bottom.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="parts">Authors, tags, and links as props</h2>
  <p><code>authors</code>, <code>links</code>, and a linked form of <code>tags</code> write those parts for you, in the order the aligned rows expect: summary, authors, tags, links. Authors are names, or <code>{"{ name, href }"}</code> to link one. <code>me</code> sets the owner's name darker, and <code>fold</code> shortens a long list to the first names, yours, and the last, with the gap read out as "more authors". A link takes <code>attrs</code> for data attributes, and a link to a PDF is kept out of hover prefetch.</p>
  <CardGrid class="ply-wide ply-cards--aligned">
    <Card href="#" title="Example title: a card written with props" meta={["Example Venue 2026"]} summary="The authors line, the tags, and the link row come from props."
      authors={["A. Author", { name: "B. Author", href: "#parts" }, "John Doe"]} me="John Doe"
      tags={[{ label: "Sheaves", href: "#parts" }, { label: "Topology", href: "#parts", current: true }]}
      links={[{ label: "Paper", href: "#" }, { label: "Code", href: "#" }, { label: "BibTeX", href: "#" }]} />
    <Card href="#" title="Example title: a second card, with a folded author list" meta={["Example Workshop 2026"]} summary="Past eight names the list folds."
      authors={["A. Author", "B. Author", "C. Author", "D. Author", "E. Author", "F. Author", "G. Author", "H. Author", "John Doe", "I. Author", "J. Author"]} me="John Doe" fold={8}
      links={[{ label: "arXiv", href: "#" }]} />
  </CardGrid>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<Card href="/papers/x/" title="..." meta={["ICLR 2026"]} summary="..."
  authors={["A. Author", { name: "B. Author", href: "/people/b/" }, "John Doe"]} me="John Doe" fold={12}
  tags={[{ label: "Sheaves", href: "/topics/sheaves/" }, "Graphs"]}
  links={[{ label: "Paper", href: "/x.pdf", attrs: { "data-track": "paper-link" } }, { label: "BibTeX", href: "/papers/x/#cite" }]} />`} />

  <h2 id="pictures">Pictures</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Write <code>sizes</code> for the column a phone shows, which is the viewport less two gutters. <code>100vw</code> overstates it by a tenth, and a phone then fetches the next size up. The helpers <code>column()</code> and <code>widthsFor()</code> from <code>@dario-loi/polytope/astro</code> write both values.</li>
    <li>A picture with a twin for the other theme takes <code>.ply-light-only</code> and <code>.ply-dark-only</code>: the one for the theme in use shows, the switch's first and then the system's, and paper takes the light one. Load the twin lazily at normal priority, since a reader sees one of the two.</li>
    <li>A flush panel that holds a picture gets a hairline frame (<code>.ply-panel--flush</code> with an <code>img</code>), so a light screenshot keeps its edge on the white wall, as a card's panel does.</li>
  </ul>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`import { Picture } from "astro:assets";
import { column, pictureFormats, widthsFor } from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro";

<Picture {...pictureFormats} src={light} widths={widthsFor(light, 720)} sizes={\`(min-width: 64rem) 30vw, \${column()}\`} class="ply-light-only" alt="" />
<Picture {...pictureFormats} src={dark} widths={widthsFor(dark, 720)} sizes={\`(min-width: 64rem) 30vw, \${column()}\`} loading="lazy" class="ply-dark-only" alt="" />`} />

  <h3 id="themed">Twins and the fallback complex</h3>
  <p><code>ThemedImage</code> writes the twin pair. <code>src</code> is an imported image, which goes through Astro's <code>Picture</code> with the widths and sizes you give, or an address, which becomes a plain <code>img</code>. The twin in <code>dark</code> loads lazily at normal priority, so the theme not in use never competes with the one on screen. A picture that fails to load is hidden.</p>
  <p>Under a picture in the <code>figure</code> slot, a card draws its complex with no script (<code>ComplexStatic</code>), and the loaded picture covers it. A picture that is slow, missing, or failed leaves the complex. The second card below points at a picture that does not exist.</p>
  <CardGrid class="ply-wide">
    <Card href="#themed" title="Example title: a screenshot with a dark twin" meta={["Example Venue 2026"]} summary="Switch the theme to see the twin.">
      <ThemedImage slot="figure" src={light} dark={dark} alt="" />
    </Card>
    <Card href="#themed" title="Example title: a picture that is missing" meta={["Example Venue 2025"]} summary="The complex shows where the picture would be.">
      <ThemedImage slot="figure" src={u("/examples/missing.webp")} alt="" />
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<Card href="/papers/x/" title="..." meta={["ICLR 2026"]}>
  <ThemedImage slot="figure" src={light} dark={dark} alt="" widths={widthsFor(light, 720)} sizes={\`(min-width: 64rem) 30vw, \${column()}\`} />
</Card>`} />

  <h2>Plain HTML</h2>
  <Code lang="html" code={`<div class="ply-cards">
  <article class="ply-card ply-card--lead">
    <a class="ply-card__panel" href="/papers/x/" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
      <img src="/x.webp" alt="" />
      <span class="ply-card__labels"><span class="ply-label ply-label--flag">Top 3 paper</span></span>
    </a>
    <div class="ply-card__body">
      <h3 class="ply-card__title"><a href="/papers/x/">Name<span class="ply-card__sub-mark">: </span><span class="ply-card__sub">what it does</span></a></h3>
      <p class="ply-card__meta"><span>ICLR 2026</span><span>Oral</span></p>
      <p class="ply-card__summary">...</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</div>`} />
</Docs>
